  "messages": [
    {"role": "user", "content": "Define the term atomic mass."},
    {"role": "assistant", "reasoning": "Learner is impatient and wants a direct answer. Use a relatable analogy to introduce the concept without giving the full definition yet. Diagnose if they understand relative measurement.", "content": "Imagine you're at a braai, and someone asks how heavy the meat is. Instead of a scale, you compare it to a known weight, like a 1-litre bottle of water. You say, 'It's about twice as heavy as the water bottle.' That's similar to how atomic mass works! It compares the mass of an atom to a standard reference. What do you think that reference might be?"},
    {"role": "user", "content": "is it like comparing to a specific atom?"},
    {"role": "assistant", "reasoning": "Learner grasped the relative comparison concept. Now guide them to identify carbon-12 as the standard. [TOOL CHECK: None needed]", "content": "Yes, exactly! Scientists use a specific atom as the standard. It's like using a 1-kilogram weight at the gym to compare all other weights. In chemistry, the standard is a carbon-12 atom. What do you think happens to its mass in this system?"},
    {"role": "user", "content": "so the carbon-12 atom is given a specific value?"},
    {"role": "assistant", "reasoning": "Learner is on the right track. Now reveal the assigned value of 12 atomic mass units and guide to the full definition. [TOOL CHECK: None needed]", "content": "Yes, the carbon-12 atom is assigned a mass of 12 atomic mass units (amu). So, when we say 'atomic mass,' we mean the mass of any atom compared to this carbon-12 standard. For example, if an atom is 24 amu, it's twice as heavy as carbon-12. Make sense?"},
    {"role": "assistant", "reasoning": "Provide the complete definition, formula breakdown, and exam tip as per the final turn structure. [TOOL CHECK: None needed]", "content": "The **atomic mass** is the mass of an atom relative to the mass of a carbon-12 atom, which is assigned a mass of 12 atomic mass units.\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** Atomic Mass = (Mass of Atom / Mass of Carbon-12) × 12 amu\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * **Atomic Mass:** The mass of the atom in atomic mass units (amu)\n  * **Mass of Atom:** The actual mass of the atom being measured\n  * **Mass of Carbon-12:** The mass of a carbon-12 atom, set to 12 amu\n* **Why we use it:** Just like comparing weights at the gym, this system gives us a consistent way to measure and compare the masses of all atoms, making it easier to work with tiny atomic scales.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always mention the carbon-12 standard and its assigned value of 12 amu in your definition. Leaving out these details will cost you marks!"}
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "analogy_real_world",
    "final_answer": "The mass of an atom relative to the mass of a carbon-12 atom, which is assigned a mass of 12 atomic mass units.",
    "verify_code": "print(\"The mass of an atom relative to the mass of a carbon-12 atom, which is assigned a mass of 12 atomic mass units.\")",
    "techniques": ["analogy_explanation", "units_check", "given_formula_substitute_answer"]
  }
